<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Paul Doumer</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1857">1857</a>–<a href="/years/1932">1932</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Paul Doumer was a French politician who served as governor-general of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">French Indochina</a> from 1897 to 1902 and as president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> from 1931 until his assassination in 1932. In Indochina he centralised administration under the governor-general, set up a general budget financed by state monopolies on opium, salt and alcohol, and began major public works, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a> railway and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanoi">Hanoi</a> bridge later named after him.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/paul-doumer.jpg" alt="Paul Doumer" width="320" height="439" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous, National Library of France · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Paul-Doumer_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Joseph Athanase Doumer, commonly known as Paul, was born on 22 March 1857 at Aurillac in the Cantal department into a family of modest means. A former student of the Conservatoire national des arts et métiers, he became a professor of mathematics at Mende in 1877 and taught at Remiremont from 1879 to 1883, leaving on health grounds to become chief editor of a regional newspaper, the Courrier de l'<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a>. In 1878 he married Blanche Richel. They had eight children, four of whom were killed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>. He was initiated into Freemasonry in 1879 and became Grand Secretary of the Grand Orient de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1892.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Doumer entered politics in 1885 as chef de cabinet to Charles Floquet, president of the Chamber of Deputies, and was elected Radical deputy for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aisne">Aisne</a> in 1888. Defeated in 1889, he was returned in 1890 by the arrondissement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auxerre">Auxerre</a>. As minister of finance in 1895–1896 he tried without success to introduce an income tax.</p><p>When he arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indochina">Indochina</a> the colonies were losing millions of francs a year, and his taxes were meant to put them on a paying basis. Vietnamese, Cambodians and Laotians who could not or would not pay lost their houses and land and often became day labourers. He made Indochina a market for French products and a field for French investment. In 1898 he persuaded the French government to approve the railway to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yunnan">Yunnan</a>, with a view to annexing the south of that province.</p><p>Back in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> he was elected deputy for Laon, refused to support the ministry of Émile Combes and led a group of dissident Radicals that eventually helped bring it down. He was president of the Chamber from 1902 to 1905, minister of finance again in 1925 and president of the Senate from 1927. On 13 May 1931 he was elected president of the Republic, defeating the better-known Aristide Briand.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 6 May 1932, at the opening of a book fair at the Hôtel Salomon de Rothschild in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Doumer was shot by Paul Gorguloff, a Russian émigré, while talking to the author Claude Farrère. He died in hospital at 04:37 on 7 May, the second French president to be assassinated and the first to be shot. Gorguloff wrote that the act was a protest on behalf of exiled White Russians and against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>'s relations with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>; he was guillotined four months later. Doumer wrote L'Indo-Chine française (1904) and Le Livre de mes fils (1906).</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Paul Doumer” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Paul Doumer</b>, who built Indochina’s federal government and the Hanoi bridge first named after him, later became President of France and was assassinated in 1932.</blockquote><a href="/empire/french-indochina">French Indochina · At a glance</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Aurillac, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=44.93,2.44&amp;year=1905" aria-label="Aurillac on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4608 577 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Paul Doumer"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5058 847h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5058 847h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=44.93,2.44&amp;year=1905">What was here in 1905? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
